# multica-local-workdir configuration overrides.
# Copy to `.env` (in this same directory) and uncomment what you need.

# Override the claude binary (default: `claude` on PATH)
# CLAUDE_BIN=/path/to/claude

# Override the opencode binary (default: `opencode` on PATH)
# OPENCODE_BIN=/path/to/opencode

# Which `pi` variant the `pi` wrapper runs: `omp` (oh-my-pi, the richer setup)
# or `pi` (vanilla). This is the operator's default; set LWD_PI_VARIANT per-agent
# in multica to override it for one agent. Unset → `pi`.
# LWD_PI_VARIANT=omp

# Binary for the `pi` variant (default: `pi` on PATH).
# PI_BIN=/path/to/pi

# Binary for the `omp` variant (default: `omp` on PATH).
# OMP_BIN=/path/to/omp

# Override the multica binary (default: `multica` on PATH).
# Only consumed by the `multica-daemon` wrapper.
# MULTICA_BIN=/path/to/multica

# WebSocket URL of the multica server.
# Only consumed by the `multica-daemon` wrapper.
# MULTICA_SERVER_URL=ws://multica.example.com/ws

# Provider selection is per-wrapper, with a shared fallback:
#   LWD_CLAUDE_PROVIDER -> the `claude` wrapper  (claude-providers/<name>.sh)
#   LWD_PI_PROVIDER     -> the `pi` wrapper, omp variant (pi-providers/<name>.yml)
#   LWD_PROVIDER        -> generic fallback read by whichever specific var is unset
# The wrapper-specific var wins; LWD_PROVIDER is the one-knob default for both.
# Within either, ambient (per-agent) env beats .env, and a task `provider:`
# label beats both (see LWD_TASK_SETTINGS). `set-claude-provider` edits LWD_CLAUDE_PROVIDER.
#
# claude-providers/<name>.sh exports ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL / auth / model env to
# redirect claude at a non-Anthropic backend (DeepSeek, Z.AI, …).
# pi-providers/<name>.yml is an omp config.yml-style overlay (model roles,
# thinking, search provider, …); the vanilla pi variant has no overlay flag and
# ignores the provider. Use the same name in both dirs (e.g. z-ai) to drive both
# wrappers via the generic LWD_PROVIDER, or set the specific vars independently.
# LWD_PROVIDER=deepseek
# LWD_CLAUDE_PROVIDER=deepseek
# LWD_PI_PROVIDER=z-ai
# LWD_MODEL=deepseek-v4-pro[1m]
# Note: on `claude`, with no provider (native Anthropic) LWD_MODEL is injected
# as `--model <value>` unless the caller already passed --model; with a provider
# active the provider file consumes LWD_MODEL instead. On `pi`, LWD_MODEL is
# always injected as `--model` (overriding any overlay's default role).

# API key for the `deepseek` provider. Required only when LWD_PROVIDER=deepseek.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...

# API key for the `openrouter` provider. Required only when LWD_PROVIDER=openrouter.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# Default model for the openrouter provider (overridden by LWD_MODEL if set).
# LWD_OPENROUTER_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.7-max

# API key for the `z-ai` provider (Z.AI / GLM, Anthropic-compatible endpoint).
# Required only when LWD_PROVIDER=z-ai.
# ZAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# The `opencode-go` provider routes claude through OpenCode Go's open-weight
# models. OpenCode Go has no Anthropic-compatible endpoint, so it needs the
# `oc-go-cc` translating proxy running separately (NOT started by these wrappers):
#   OC_GO_CC_API_KEY=sk-opencode-... oc-go-cc serve   # listens on 127.0.0.1:3456
# Then set LWD_PROVIDER=opencode-go. The subscription key lives in the proxy's
# env above, not here. Override the proxy URL only if you ran it on another port:
# OPENCODE_GO_PROXY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3456
# Tier→model mapping (default/thinking/long-context/background) is configured in
# oc-go-cc itself, not via LWD_MODEL.

# Force a `--fallback-model` on the `claude` wrapper (skipped if the caller
# already passes `--fallback-model`). claude accepts a comma-separated list and
# tries each in order. Handy when switching providers whose models reject some
# request parameters (thinking, …) — claude retries on the fallback. The model
# strings are passed verbatim to whatever backend claude points at, so they can
# be provider-specific (OpenRouter/DeepSeek/etc.), not just Anthropic IDs.
# Priority: ambient env > provider file > .env.
# LWD_FALLBACK_MODEL=glm-5.1:cloud,claude-opus-4-8,claude-sonnet-4-6,deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro

# Set the reasoning effort. On the `claude` wrapper this injects `--effort
# <level>` (low, medium, high, xhigh, max). On the `pi` wrapper the same knob
# injects `--thinking <level>` — pi/omp's reasoning-level flag — accepting
# minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh (max is claude-only and dropped on pi). Both
# inject unless the caller already passed the flag, and both fail loud on a
# typo. Set this PER-AGENT in multica for dynamic effort classification: give
# each agent the effort its job warrants (e.g. max for orchestration/review,
# low for trivial chores). Priority: ambient env > provider file > .env.
# For PER-TASK effort, put an `effort: <level>` label on the issue (see
# LWD_TASK_SETTINGS below) — that overrides this.
# LWD_EFFORT=high

# Per-task settings: the `claude` and `pi` wrappers read `key: value` labels
# from the issue (effort/model/provider/path), which outrank per-agent and
# global settings. On by default; needs `jq`. It runs one `multica issue get`
# per launch (~0.3s) — set to 0/off/false/no to disable.
# LWD_TASK_SETTINGS=0

# Strip `--strict-mcp-config` from claude's argv so the project's MCP servers
# (`.claude/settings.json`, user config) load. multica injects that flag to
# sandbox SaaS agents; on a self-hosted setup you usually want it gone.
# Only opt in if you trust every MCP server the project can reach.
# LWD_ALLOW_MCP=1
